Thanks Mike, that makes sense! One more question. With the endoR function from NicheMapR, is it able to simulate temperature-dependent torpor (or hibernation; i.e., pronounced reduction, but maintained, physiological states like metabolic rate and EWL; see attached image for example) for a small-bodied mammal? For example, I would like to simulate yearly changes in torpor duration and overall energy expenditure under different climate models. During the spring-summer season, the animal does its usual thing, then in autumn-winter, it enters torpor after reaching below a body temperature threshold (e.g., normal body temp reduces by 5°C). Is there a way to simulate this?

I had a look at your paper Kearney (2019) How will snow alter exposure of organisms to cold under climate change, which talks about hibernation cost. But in the methods, the simulation ran for "a hibernating 20 g endotherm at 5 cm depth" and "Basal metabolic rate was adjusted from the expected normothermic value at 37°C". So it seems that the simulated endotherm was in a hibernating state all year?

Regards,
Nicholas

